WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:04.580
You know her as Elizabeth Shaw, the intrepid
scientist who makes a brief appearance in

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:09.360
Ridley Scott's Alien: Covenant after starring
in its predecessor, Prometheus.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:14.009
But the woman who plays her — Swedish actress
Noomi Rapace — has been heating up Hollywood

00:00:14.009 --> 00:00:16.340
since her breakout in 2009.

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:19.570
Here are all the places you've seen her before.

00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:21.740
The Millennium Trilogy

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:26.160
Thanks to director David Fincher and a $90
million Hollywood budget, you've probably

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:31.279
heard of The Girl with the Dragon Tattoo — and
its titular protagonist, the brooding, tattooed

00:00:31.279 --> 00:00:32.829
Lisbeth Salander.

00:00:32.829 --> 00:00:37.370
But two years before Rooney Mara earned an
Oscar nomination for playing Stieg Larsson's

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:41.950
heroine, Noomi Rapace played the role to great
acclaim in her native Sweden.

00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:46.239
In fact, it's unlikely that Lisbeth Salander
would have ever gotten a big-budget outing

00:00:46.239 --> 00:00:51.289
in mainstream Hollywood if the Swedish actress
hadn't turned in such a stunner of a performance

00:00:51.289 --> 00:00:52.289
first.

00:00:52.289 --> 00:00:56.969
While The Girl with the Dragon Tattoo is pretty
much a household name, Stieg Larsson's Millennium

00:00:56.969 --> 00:00:59.710
book series was actually adapted as a trilogy.

00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:04.680
Noomi Rapace reprised her role as Lisbeth
Salander in two more movies, The Girl Who

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:08.040
Played with Fire and The Girl Who Kicked the
Hornet's Nest.

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:12.200
All told, Rapace spent a full year playing
Lisbeth, a performance that resulted in three

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:17.850
feature films, six television episodes' worth
of raw material, and rave reviews across the

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:18.850
board.

00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:21.270
However, once she was done, she was really
done.

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:24.009
In an interview with NPR, the actress said:

00:01:24.009 --> 00:01:25.909
"[It] was kind of strange.

00:01:25.909 --> 00:01:30.390
You know the last day, after [we filmed] the
last scene, all the producers came with champagne

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:32.409
and everybody wanted to celebrate.

00:01:32.409 --> 00:01:36.360
And I just said, 'I need to go to the bathroom,'
and I just started to throw up.

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:40.840
It was like my body was physically throwing
Lisbeth out."

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:42.290
The Monitor

00:01:42.290 --> 00:01:46.540
Compared to Rapace's work in The Girl with
the Dragon Tattoo, the Swedish horror film

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:50.130
The Monitor was pretty low-profile, except
in Sweden.

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:54.689
But fans of the genre will recognize it — and
more importantly, its leading lady, for her

00:01:54.689 --> 00:01:58.290
pitch-perfect performance as a woman on the
brink of insanity.

00:01:58.290 --> 00:02:02.930
The Monitor is an understated slow burner
featuring the creepiest home appliance ever:

00:02:02.930 --> 00:02:04.200
the baby monitor.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:08.970
Rapace is memorable as a traumatized single
mother who just wants to listen to her kid

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:13.990
while he sleeps, but gets an earful of something
sinister instead.

00:02:13.990 --> 00:02:16.190
Sherlock Holmes: A Game of Shadows

00:02:16.190 --> 00:02:21.060
For those who hadn't already discovered Noomi
Rapace via the Dragon Tattoo trilogy, there

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:26.740
was no missing her in her big-budget debut
alongside Robert Downey, Jr. and Jude Law.

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:31.560
Rapace played Madame Simza in Sherlock Holmes:
A Game of Shadows, channeling her trademark

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:36.690
ferocity into the role of a Romani gypsy woman
who gets caught up in a deadly conspiracy

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:39.990
spearheaded by Sherlock's archnemesis Moriarty.

00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:44.380
And her performance is doubly impressive because
she had to cross a huge language barrier to

00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:45.380
do it.

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:47.170
Rapace revealed in an interview:

00:02:47.170 --> 00:02:51.930
"I didn't speak English like three years ago,
and for me to find a way to be free and be

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:53.840
able to live in this language…

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:57.620
instead of [feeling] like you're stuck in
this prison of control where you can do perfect

00:02:57.620 --> 00:03:02.300
lines but you can't improvise and you can't
ad-lib ... after a week I'd forgotten about

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:03.300
it.

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:04.300
It's the way they work.

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:07.160
Those boys are fantastic."

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:08.160
Dead Man Down

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:13.450
Of course you recognize Noomi Rapace from
Prometheus, but after leaving the Alien franchise,

00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:18.480
Noomi Rapace reunited with Niels Arden Oplev
— the man who helmed the original Swedish

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:21.910
The Girl with the Dragon Tattoo — for Dead
Man Down

00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:26.730
In addition to Rapace, Dead Man Down also
boasts a cast that includes Colin Farrell,

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:28.900
Dominic Cooper, and Terrence Howard.

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:33.450
Unfortunately, despite all that star power,
the movie itself wasn't particularly well

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:35.510
received...by anyone.

00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:40.810
One critic at Variety called it "revenge-fantasy
dreck" and "a derivative collection of brazen

00:03:40.810 --> 00:03:43.400
plot holes and late-night-cable cliches."

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:45.350
The Drop

00:03:45.350 --> 00:03:50.120
Adapted by Dennis Lehane from his own short
story, "Animal Rescue," The Drop is a quiet

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:55.120
film noir about crime, vengeance, and decent
men who do very bad things in the basements

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:56.480
of Brooklyn bars.

00:03:56.480 --> 00:04:00.910
And for Noomi Rapace, it presented a serious
challenge: namely, having to hold her own

00:04:00.910 --> 00:04:06.090
onscreen against not just Tom Hardy, who'd
just become super famous after doing Inception

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:12.450
and The Dark Knight Rises back-to-back, but
also the world's most adorable pit bull puppy.

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:14.360
Child 44

00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:18.970
Perhaps thanks to her incredible chemistry
with Tom Hardy in The Drop, Noomi Rapace turned

00:04:18.970 --> 00:04:23.790
up immediately in another movie with the British
actor, this time as his wife in the Soviet-era

00:04:23.790 --> 00:04:25.570
thriller Child 44.

00:04:25.570 --> 00:04:30.380
In it, Hardy plays disgraced Stalin security
officer Leo Demidov, who tries to track a

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:33.130
serial killer against the wishes of his government.

00:04:33.130 --> 00:04:37.180
Though this film didn't get as much attention
as some of Rapace's other projects thanks

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:41.840
to a limited release in the U.S., everyone
turned in solid performances that made for

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:46.790
a tense, effective political thriller… even
if everyone in it struggles occasionally with

00:04:46.790 --> 00:04:48.690
their Russian accents.

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:49.710
Thanks for watching!

00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:52.730
